import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a7 as s}from"./chunks/framework.C0-dE_fW.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Project Maturity","description":"How VERA judges a project on a ladder of steps, from set up to profitable, and why each step depends on the one below it.","frontmatter":{"description":"How VERA judges a project on a ladder of steps, from set up to profitable, and why each step depends on the one below it."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md","filePath":"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md"}'),n={name:"reference/concepts/project-maturity.md"};function i(r,e,h,d,l,p){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="project-maturity" tabindex="-1">Project Maturity <a class="header-anchor" href="#project-maturity" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project Maturity&quot;">​</a></h1><p>VERA judges a project against a ladder. Each step is a gate: it cannot be assessed at one step until every step below it is satisfied.</p><p>The ladder is ordered by <strong>how much VERA can truthfully say</strong> about a project — not by what to do next, which is a separate question the advice on each project page answers.</p><h2 id="the-ladder" tabindex="-1">The ladder <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-ladder" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The ladder&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Name</th><th>Reached when</th><th>What it makes answerable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td>Nominal</td><td>the project exists</td><td>nothing</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Owned</td><td>it has a project manager</td><td>accountability, and it appears in manager comparisons</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Scoped</td><td>it has a deadline and an hours budget, and a gross profit if it is fixed price</td><td>&quot;late&quot; and &quot;over&quot; become meaningful; the burn-up, budget health and revenue all have something to measure against</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Staffed</td><td>at least one person has an allocation</td><td>committed capacity, and the allocation reading on the page</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Tracking</td><td>it is on course, within budget health, and will not run out of hours before its deadline</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Profitable</td><td>it is earning at or above the target ratio, with healthy margin and billing in step</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Closed well</td><td>it finished on time and on budget, and the plan it was measured against turned out to be right</td><td>the retrospective verdict</td></tr></tbody></table><p>A project&#39;s step is the highest one for which every step below it is also satisfied. An allocation&#39;s hours spread evenly over the project&#39;s dates; VERA asks for neither dates of their own nor a week-by-week plan.</p><h2 id="recorded-time-is-a-gate-not-a-step" tabindex="-1">Recorded time is a gate, not a step <a class="header-anchor" href="#recorded-time-is-a-gate-not-a-step" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Recorded time is a gate, not a step&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Time is recorded from the first day, often before anyone has been allocated, so it is not a step of its own. It is a precondition on steps 4 and above: without recorded time those are <strong>unanswerable</strong>, which is not the same as failing. A brand-new project is not behind schedule; nothing is yet known about it.</p><h2 id="what-step-4-says-and-what-it-will-not-say" tabindex="-1">What step 4 says, and what it will not say <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-step-4-says-and-what-it-will-not-say" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What step 4 says, and what it will not say&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Once a project is planned and time is being recorded against it, VERA can tell whether it is tracking. It reports on that in a deliberately narrow way.</p><p><strong>When nothing at all has been recorded.</strong> If someone has an allocation here, weeks that have already finished expected hours from them, and nothing whatsoever has been recorded against the project, VERA names that person and says so. That is a gap in what VERA knows, not a statement about the person: the work may have been done and not logged. The wording says exactly that, and it is only ever shown to the people who run the project — never to the person it names.</p><p><strong>When time is recorded but differs from the plan.</strong> Each allocation&#39;s hours are expected evenly across its weeks. VERA says the project as a whole is above or below that pace, and points at the per-person view. It does not name anybody, because a plan that was not followed is as often a plan that was wrong.</p><p><strong>Never a ranking.</strong> VERA does not say who is furthest behind, does not order people by how far off they are, and uses no superlatives about anybody.</p><p><strong>Only finished weeks count.</strong> The week in progress is never judged, which is also why this does not change from one day to the next — it changes when a week closes.</p><p><strong>Pace is only raised when it is serious.</strong> The project page already reports schedule slippage as soon as it passes ten percent. Advice waits until twice that, so it stays quieter than the reading it sits above rather than repeating it.</p><h2 id="what-step-6-says-vera-s-verdict-on-a-finished-project" tabindex="-1">What step 6 says: VERA&#39;s verdict on a finished project <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-step-6-says-vera-s-verdict-on-a-finished-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What step 6 says: VERA&#39;s verdict on a finished project&quot;">​</a></h2><p>When a project is completed, a card at the top of its page says what VERA makes of it, worked out from the project&#39;s own data with no AI involved. It asks about money and time, then whether the project ended on time.</p><p><strong>Money</strong> compares the ratio with the target: strong, healthy, thin or lost money. A ratio three times the target or more reads &quot;check the costs&quot;, because that usually means a cost isn&#39;t in VERA. The same people see this as see the figure: owners, admins and Manager Plus in full, the project&#39;s manager by ratio only, and other managers not at all.</p><p><strong>Time</strong> compares recorded hours with the budget, within 10% or beyond. Lateness is not counted here; it has its own part.</p><p><strong>On time</strong> asks whether the project finished by its original deadline. Moving the deadline later doesn&#39;t rescue it: the date it was first due is the one that counts. Finishing on or before it is an A. Finishing after it is scaled by how late it ran, as a share of the time from the project&#39;s start to that original date: up to 20% late is a B, up to 40% a C, up to 60% a D, and beyond that an F. A project with no deadline can&#39;t be judged on this, so that part of the grade is left out and the others share its weight. A running project is judged only once it is past its original deadline with the work still open; before then it reads that the deadline hasn&#39;t passed yet. The card says it in one plain line, naming the date.</p><p><strong>The headline</strong> puts the two together. &quot;A good result, but it finished late&quot; means the money and hours came out well but the project ran past its original deadline. &quot;On time, but it missed&quot; points at the price or the estimate rather than the schedule.</p><p>It is a judgment, and it says so: it writes &quot;likely&quot;, and it never names a person. A cancelled project gets its facts and no verdict.</p><h2 id="what-step-5-says-and-who-hears-it" tabindex="-1">What step 5 says, and who hears it <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-step-5-says-and-who-hears-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What step 5 says, and who hears it&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Step 5 asks whether the project earns enough for the work going into it. It is the one part of the ladder where who is looking changes what is said.</p><p><strong>Owners see it in full.</strong> They already see what a project earns and what it costs, so nothing here is new to them.</p><p><strong>The project&#39;s manager sees the ratio, and only the ratio.</strong> Someone who runs a project already sees how much revenue it earns per dollar of work — that has been true since the figure appeared beside their own projects in the sidebar. Step 5 says the same thing in words. It never shows them what the project is worth, what it costs, or what anyone is paid.</p><p><strong>Everyone else is told nothing at all.</strong> A manager who does not run this project cannot see the figure, so VERA does not comment on it. The distinction that matters: the project is recorded as <em>unknown</em>, never as <em>unprofitable</em>. Saying a project is losing money to somebody who cannot see whether it is would be a confident claim about data they do not have.</p><p><strong>A project nobody has priced is not judged.</strong> A fixed price project with no gross profit, or a time and materials project with no billing rate, earns nothing on paper — so its ratio would read as a disaster when the truth is that nobody has said what the work is worth. VERA says <em>that</em> instead, and says it only to an owner.</p><p><strong>Advice is quieter than the reading above it.</strong> The project page shows this ratio as a colored pill. Advice stays silent for as long as that pill is green, and speaks only once it is not — the same rule step 6 follows by raising pace at twice the threshold the schedule reading uses.</p><p><strong>Two different bars, deliberately.</strong> Reaching step 5 means hitting the target. Being advised about it means falling well below the target. A project a little short of target is doing fine and is left alone; it simply has not earned the step yet.</p><p><strong>Losing money today and closing at a loss are different things.</strong> A project can be earning its keep so far and still be heading for a loss once every allocated hour is logged and every supplier is paid. VERA says that separately, to owners, and stops repeating itself when the project is already losing money today.</p><p><strong>Nobody is named at step 5, ever.</strong> It is a statement about a contract and the hours booked against it.</p><h2 id="retainers-the-one-thing-said-about-the-engagement-itself" tabindex="-1">Retainers: the one thing said about the engagement itself <a class="header-anchor" href="#retainers-the-one-thing-said-about-the-engagement-itself" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Retainers: the one thing said about the engagement itself&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A retainer engagement stops at step 1, because each of its periods is assessed on its own. One thing about the engagement as a whole is still worth saying: when the current period is about to end and no next period exists, the advice says the renewal is due and points at the Periods card. It is the same test as the renewal message VERA sends that morning, so the two always agree. An owner is also told when people with allocations on the project have no cost rate, because their hours then cost nothing and LER reads too high; the link goes to the Rates tab.</p><h2 id="not-every-project-should-reach-the-top" tabindex="-1">Not every project should reach the top <a class="header-anchor" href="#not-every-project-should-reach-the-top" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Not every project should reach the top&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A project&#39;s kind sets how far up the ladder it can meaningfully climb, and stopping at that point means it is configured correctly — not that something is missing.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Stops at</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internal, and all time-off projects</td><td>Owned</td><td>they carry no budget, deadline or allocations by design</td></tr><tr><td>Pro bono</td><td>Tracking</td><td>they earn nothing by definition, so step 5 would read as total failure on every one of them</td></tr><tr><td>Time and materials, fixed price</td><td>Closed well</td><td>the full ladder applies</td></tr><tr><td>Retainer and agile engagements</td><td>Owned</td><td>the engagement itself holds no period values; each period is assessed in full on its own</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Without these ceilings the advice would nag forever about projects that are set up exactly as intended.</p><h2 id="the-one-line-headline" tabindex="-1">The one-line headline <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-one-line-headline" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The one-line headline&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each project page opens with a single line that answers &quot;does this project need me?&quot;:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Headline</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Needs setup</strong></td><td>Something must be configured before VERA can judge the project, such as a missing budget, deadline or allocation.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Needs attention</strong></td><td>Something is wrong now: the project is off course, running out early, losing money or past its deadline.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>No action needed</strong></td><td>The project is on course and nothing is waiting on you. Any suggestions below it are optional.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The headline has no rules of its own. It is read from the advice beneath it and from the project&#39;s schedule verdict, so it can never disagree with them. A finished project, a time-off project such as PTO, and a project with no time recorded yet show no headline, because there is nothing to judge.</p><h2 id="where-advice-appears" tabindex="-1">Where advice appears <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-advice-appears" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where advice appears&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the project page, every item. In Needs you, only the blocking items of projects that need setup. Blocking rules read setup facts alone, which one bulk read can supply for every project at once, so the list there agrees exactly with the project pages it links to. Attention advice needs each project&#39;s own analytics, so it is not listed in Needs you.</p><h2 id="keeping-this-current" tabindex="-1">Keeping this current <a class="header-anchor" href="#keeping-this-current" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Keeping this current&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This page is the contract. The advice on each project page cites the step each piece of advice belongs to, and a test checks that every cited step exists here. When VERA gains a new capability it either belongs to a step already on this ladder, or it extends the top — it never quietly reorders the middle.</p>',46)])])}const u=t(n,[["render",i]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
